 ## Background
-The field originated with methods like [D-NeRF](8660-d-nerf.md), which established the canonical space paradigm for dynamic NeRFs. As 3D Gaussian Splatting gained popularity, researchers adapted it for dynamic scenes, leading to explicit deformation methods like Dynamic 3D Gaussian Splatting (using Fourier series) and Temporal 3D Gaussian Splatting (using polynomial functions for motion and rotation) [S1]. More recent approaches like [SWAG](6875-swag.md) focus on handling transient objects, while SWAGS handles complex motion windows within the Gaussian framework [S1]. Applications extend to medical fields, such as markless motion reconstruction for infants and monocular endoscopic reconstruction [S1].
+Dynamic 3D reconstruction has evolved from explicit multi-view stereo to implicit neural representations. D-NeRF [S1] was a foundational work in decomposing dynamic scenes into canonical and deformation fields. The advent of 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1] provided a more efficient explicit representation, leading to dynamic variants like Dynamic 3DGS [S2] and SWAG [S1]. These methods address the limitations of NeRF-based approaches, such as slow training and rendering, by leveraging the explicit nature of Gaussians while incorporating temporal modeling techniques [S1]. Applications include medical imaging, such as motion analysis of infants and monocular endoscopic reconstruction [S1].
 
